          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a code must never be sequenced first, how is that shown in the classification?",
          "answer": "The passages I retrieved do not explain how a code that must never be sequenced first is marked. They don't mention manifestation codes, asterisk or dagger codes, or the \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" notes as a category, so I can't give you that rule from these sources.\n\nHere is what they do say:\n\n- **Where the instructions live:** The index does not carry the instructional notes. The tabular list does, and it governs. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n- **Note types:** An Excludes1 note means two conditions cannot both be present and their codes are never reported together. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n- **Sequencing in practice:** Some categories, such as anemia in Chapter 3, carry sequencing instructions that point to codes in other chapters. (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 3: blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism*)\n- **Paired codes:** Diabetic complications often need a combination code plus an additional code for detail, such as the stage of kidney disease. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*)\n\nFor the specific marking convention, check the tabular list and its introductory notes in the current official ICD-10-CM release. Coding rules change by year, so use the edition that applies to your work.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
